# CPU Project Rules and Important Information

## Environment basics
- Don't directly run sbt, we do not use sbt.
- Mill build tool is installed and available on the users PATH, do not run it with ./mill. But simply 'mill ...'.
- The CPU is implemented in Scala using the Chisel library

## Commands
- Run CPU tests with trace output: `bash tools/run_cpu_with_trace.sh`
  - This generates trace files at:
    - CPU raw trace: `trace/cpu_trace.txt`
    - Annotated trace: `trace/annotated_trace.txt`
- If you want to build for the FPGA itself then run `./build.sh`, on successful completion kindly ask the user to perform the "Gowin-fu"
- Don't try running mill directly for subsetting the test cases either, simply run the tests as mentioned in the first command so we get full trace output.
- Whenever you want to verify the code you just wrote compiles, please make a call to `./build.sh` in the project directory.

## Important Notes
- Ensure that when adding new instructions the `tools/trace_analyzer.py` script is updated accordingly or traces will stop making any sense.

## Architecture Notes
- This CPU implements a subset of the 2018 RISC5 architecture (Project Oberon), it is NOT RELATED to the RISC-V architecture.
- The ISA is described in the file `docs/risc_5_arch.txt`
- The memory map is described in the file `docs/memory_map.txt`
- When making references to the ISA, refer to the above link, do NOT make up your own interpretations of the instructions or use our current implementation as the reference!
- The CPU is designed to be synthesized into an the Tang Nano 4K FPGA
- Tests are written in Scala using the ChiselTest library, a good example test is `src/test/scala/gcd/CPUSubset0Spec.scala`